ARP (Address Resolution Protocol)
PURPOSE: Maps IP addresses → MAC addresses

Your PC wants to send a packet to a remote server (e.g., 203.0.113.1):

Step 1: "I know the destination IP."
Step 2: "But I need the MAC address to send it."
Step 3: "Let me ask: Who has 203.0.113.1? Tell me your MAC!" ← THIS IS ARP!
Step 4: The router replies: "I know how to get there! My MAC is aa:bb:cc:11:22:33."
Step 5: Packet goes to the router, and the router forwards it.

WHY IT MATTERS:
Without ARP, your PC can't send ANY packets.
All traffic goes through your local router first.
ARP cache speeds up communication by storing previous lookups.
Packet Example – The Most Important One

Packet A (Request):
Sender MAC (Vendor_A) → Destination MAC (Vendor_B)
ARP Request: "Who has IP 192.168.1.100? Tell 192.168.1.1."

What's happening: The router is asking, "Who has IP 192.168.1.100?" (that’s your device).

Packet B (Reply):
Sender MAC (Vendor_B) → Destination MAC (Vendor_A)
ARP Reply: "192.168.1.100 is at aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff."
What's happening: Your device responds, "That's me! Here's my MAC address."

ARP in Real Networks
ARP Announcements (Gratuitous ARP)
Device: "I'm using this IP!"
Sender IP = Target IP (same address).
Used for IP conflict detection (checks if another device is using the same IP).
ARP Requests ("Who has?")
Router/device: "Who has IP X? Tell me!"
Sent to broadcast MAC (ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff) so all devices on the local network hear it.
ARP Replies ("It's me!")
Device: "That's me! Here's my MAC."
Sent directly to the asking device (unicast).
